WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.480
STATE OF SAMSUNG:
Samsung’s operating profits&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:01.480 --> 00:00:05.960
are falling off a cliff. Over the past year,&nbsp;
their quarterly profits have plummeted from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.960 --> 00:00:13.800
just over $10 billion down to just $490 million.&nbsp;
This represents a 95% cliff dive within just a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:20.680
matter of a year and it’s the lowest numbers&nbsp;
that Samsung has reported since 2009. Normally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.680 --> 00:00:25.680
this would be indicative of a failing company&nbsp;
like Nokia or Yahoo who’s numbers are down across&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:30.680
the board but that’s not quite the case&nbsp;
with Samsung. Take their top line numbers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.680 --> 00:00:36.880
for example. This too is down for Samsung from&nbsp;
$240 billion per year to $200 billion per year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.880 --> 00:00:44.720
but that’s a much more modest drop of about 16% as&nbsp;
opposed to 95%. A similar story can be seen with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.720 --> 00:00:49.760
their smartphone shipments as well. While they&nbsp;
have been slowly bleeding dominance and sales,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.760 --> 00:00:55.760
nothing drastic has unfolded within the past&nbsp;
12 to 24 months. This puts Samsung in a really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.760 --> 00:01:02.320
weird situation where they’re not exactly losing&nbsp;
relevance but they are losing profitability. It’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.320 --> 00:01:08.440
easy to write this off as just a market downturn&nbsp;
but when we peel back the layers, it becomes clear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.440 --> 00:01:15.000
that Samsung is facing a much larger crisis&nbsp;
due to the era of peak smartphone. So, join&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:29.393
me as we dive deeper into Samsung’s existential&nbsp;
crisis and their struggle to remain profitable.

00:01:29.393 --> 00:01:31.720
ALL IN:
Speaking of a struggle to remain profitable,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.720 --> 00:01:36.880
many investors are struggling to remain profitable&nbsp;
in this era of high inflation and high interest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.880 --> 00:01:42.000
rates. More than ever, people are hoarding cash in&nbsp;
savings accounts and brokerage accounts to earn as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:48.240
much interest as possible which is by no means a&nbsp;
bad strategy but it wont last forever. When the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:53.640
Fed starts cutting rates, savings account interest&nbsp;
will naturally follow suit. One way to overcome&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.640 --> 00:01:58.720
this decline, however, is to take advantage&nbsp;
of bonds. With bonds, you can lock in current&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.720 --> 00:02:04.600
high interest rates for not just the next 6 to&nbsp;
12 months but for as long as 30 years. If that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.600 --> 00:02:09.280
sounds enticing, please consider checking&nbsp;
out our upcoming bond investing app, Silo,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.280 --> 00:02:14.560
in the description below. But anyway, a simple&nbsp;
Google search would tell you that the primary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.560 --> 00:02:19.880
culprit behind Samsung’s dwindling profits is&nbsp;
the memory chip market. If you didn’t know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.880 --> 00:02:25.760
Samsung is not just a big memory chip producer,&nbsp;
they are the biggest memory chip producer. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.760 --> 00:02:31.280
title definitely sounds impressive on paper but&nbsp;
Samsung actually had to make massive sacrifices&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.280 --> 00:02:36.600
to get here like overbetting on the chip market.&nbsp;
You see, one of the ways that Samsung was able&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.600 --> 00:02:42.160
to beat the competition and rise to the to top&nbsp;
was simply outspending the competition. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.160 --> 00:02:48.520
they’ve been doing this for decades dating as&nbsp;
far back as 1997. This year, Samsung opened&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.520 --> 00:02:55.480
up a $1.3 billion memory chip fab in Austin TX.&nbsp;
Over the next 14 years, this would grow to being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.480 --> 00:03:00.880
a $9 billion investment which is not only one&nbsp;
of the largest chip investments in the world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.880 --> 00:03:06.440
but one of the largest foreign investments the&nbsp;
US has ever seen. This sort of spending is what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.440 --> 00:03:13.440
allowed Samsung to catch up to and overtake the&nbsp;
competition but this is also a very dangerous game&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.440 --> 00:03:19.280
because there is no moat when it comes to internal&nbsp;
components. You see, most end consumers don’t care&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.280 --> 00:03:24.760
or even know what brand of chips is going into&nbsp;
their smartphones and computers and cars. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.760 --> 00:03:29.880
there’s virtually no brand loyalty and companies&nbsp;
like Apple just go with whoever is offering the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.880 --> 00:03:36.360
best performance and price at the time. Over the&nbsp;
past decade, this has very much been Samsung but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.360 --> 00:03:41.520
it doesn’t take long to fall behind within the&nbsp;
chip industry. Moore’s law may more may not be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.520 --> 00:03:48.760
dead but one thing is for sure, chips are still&nbsp;
advancing extremely fast. Even stagnating for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.760 --> 00:03:53.840
an year or two could destroy the lead that&nbsp;
took decades to build. So, year after year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.840 --> 00:03:59.280
these chip producers are having to invest&nbsp;
more and more. Really, all of this is just one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.280 --> 00:04:05.040
massive game of chicken. Fortunately, Samsung&nbsp;
hasn’t dropped the ball when it comes to R&amp;D,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.040 --> 00:04:09.400
they’ve actually been doing the exact opposite,&nbsp;
they’ve been stepping up to the plate. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.400 --> 00:04:15.920
this has simply led to ridiculous investments.&nbsp;
Just earlier this year, Samsung announced that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.920 --> 00:04:23.280
they would be investing, wait for it, $228 billion&nbsp;
to build the world’s largest chip facility. Let me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.280 --> 00:04:28.800
put this in perspective. That’s more than Apple&nbsp;
has spent on R&amp;D across the entire company since&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.800 --> 00:04:33.960
the launch of the iPhone itself. It’s hard to find&nbsp;
data about Apple’s R&amp;D spending throughout the 80s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.960 --> 00:04:39.600
and 90s but it’s very likely that $228 billion&nbsp;
is more than Apple has spent on R&amp;D throughout&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.600 --> 00:04:46.240
their entire history. This honestly isn’t that&nbsp;
surprising given that $228 billion is enough money&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.240 --> 00:04:52.240
to buy out a fortune 50 company cash. And Samsung&nbsp;
is looking to spend this much money on an industry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.240 --> 00:04:58.920
that they could lose at anytime within just a&nbsp;
matter of 2 years. This is no doubt a gutsy bet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.920 --> 00:05:03.640
but when you take a closer look at the companies&nbsp;
internals, it starts to become more clear why&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.640 --> 00:05:09.960
Samsung is basically betting it all. It’s likely&nbsp;
that Samsung feels that they have no other choice.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.960 --> 00:05:15.120
This is probably a bit confusing because most&nbsp;
of us see Samsung as a giant conglomerate with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.120 --> 00:05:20.360
strongholds in dozens of industries. They make&nbsp;
everything from smartphones, chips, monitors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.360 --> 00:05:26.000
laptops, and TVs to refrigerators, dishwashers,&nbsp;
washing machines, and who knows what else. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.000 --> 00:05:31.120
while they do indeed have their hands in&nbsp;
every business you can think of, almost 60%&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.120 --> 00:05:38.120
of their profits came from just one sector:&nbsp;
semiconductors. So, it’s not surprising why&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.120 --> 00:05:43.480
Samsung is willing to make such a massive gamble&nbsp;
chip fabrication. It’s their bread and butter and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.480 --> 00:05:48.960
they can’t afford to lose. But, one thing they’re&nbsp;
forgetting is that just because they stay on top&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.960 --> 00:05:59.480
of the chip industry doesn't mean that they’ll&nbsp;
actually stay on top overall. Let me explain.

00:05:59.480 --> 00:06:03.200
VOLATILITY STRIKES:
If large amounts of capital&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.200 --> 00:06:08.840
and a lack of a moat weren’t bad enough, another&nbsp;
major pitfall of the chip industry is that it’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.840 --> 00:06:14.400
one of the most cyclical industries in the world&nbsp;
and pandemic simply exacerbated this effect. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.400 --> 00:06:21.120
think we all remember how tech was a bit hard to&nbsp;
come by back in 2020 and 2021. PS5s and graphics&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.120 --> 00:06:27.360
cards were basically impossible to come by. Often&nbsp;
times you had to pay 2-3 times the price just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.360 --> 00:06:32.800
to buy these gadgets and it wasn’t just tech&nbsp;
products either. Anything that even slightly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.800 --> 00:06:37.840
had to do with chips were extremely hard to&nbsp;
come by including cars and even refrigerators&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.840 --> 00:06:43.000
which often had waiting times in the 6-12 month&nbsp;
range. As such, all of these products naturally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.000 --> 00:06:48.680
rocketed in price. Most people just figured that&nbsp;
these price increases where due to high inflation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.680 --> 00:06:53.800
but a lot of it just had to do with the global&nbsp;
chip shortage. You see, when the pandemic hit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.800 --> 00:06:58.840
most chip fabricators either pulled back or&nbsp;
completely shut down temporarily due to covid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.840 --> 00:07:03.760
restrictions and general uncertainty. At the same&nbsp;
time, the demand for these products went through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.760 --> 00:07:08.280
the roof. Everyone was staying at home meaning&nbsp;
that they were more dependent on electronics&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.280 --> 00:07:14.120
than ever. Moreover, they weren’t spending any&nbsp;
money on vacations or eating out or any of that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.120 --> 00:07:19.240
so they were able to spend a lot more on tech.&nbsp;
This led to this extreme situation where there was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.240 --> 00:07:25.800
no supply and massive demand leading to ridiculous&nbsp;
revenue and profit margins for chip makers. Many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.800 --> 00:07:30.960
chip makers including Samsung assumed that this&nbsp;
was simply the new norm for the modern hybrid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.960 --> 00:07:37.360
economy but it hasn’t quite played out that way.&nbsp;
In fact prices for tech products are very much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.360 --> 00:07:43.520
falling off a cliff as supply and demand have more&nbsp;
or less normalized. Take Tesla prices for example.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.520 --> 00:07:49.120
In the peak of the chip shortage in late 2021&nbsp;
and early 2022, Model X prices reached as high&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.120 --> 00:07:57.640
as $108,000. But, over the past 18 months, prices&nbsp;
have almost halved down to $63,000. This is great&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.640 --> 00:08:04.760
for consumers as some prices are finally returning&nbsp;
to reality but for Samsung, this is a nightmare.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.760 --> 00:08:11.320
In fact, normalizing supply and demand have led to&nbsp;
DRAM prices plummeting by 53% year over year. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.320 --> 00:08:16.520
experts don’t think the pain is over either, so&nbsp;
we could very well by looking at a 60% plus price&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.520 --> 00:08:22.360
drop before things finally stabilize. Given that&nbsp;
60% of Samsung’s profits were coming from chips,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.360 --> 00:08:26.720
it’s no wonder why their bottom line has been&nbsp;
obliterated. The reality is that profits from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.720 --> 00:08:31.640
their chip business have not only gone down,&nbsp;
they’ve actually become negative. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.640 --> 00:08:37.720
over the first 6 months of 2023, Samsung took a&nbsp;
$7 billion loss on their chip business. Samsung&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.720 --> 00:08:42.400
is of course trying to cut production to prop&nbsp;
up prices as much as they can but unfortunately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.400 --> 00:08:47.480
demand is falling even faster. You see, it’s&nbsp;
not just prices that have been plummeting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.480 --> 00:08:52.080
demand has also been plummeting as companies and&nbsp;
consumers have become much more conservative with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.080 --> 00:08:55.840
tech spending due to high inflation&nbsp;
and recession fears. Not to mention,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.840 --> 00:09:01.080
most people just got a bunch of new tech over&nbsp;
the past few years so they don’t exactly feel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.080 --> 00:09:13.377
the need to upgrade which brings us into a&nbsp;
bigger question: Will Samsung ever recover?

00:09:13.377 --> 00:09:16.280
ERA OF PEAK SMARTPHONE:
Normally, the answer would just be yes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.280 --> 00:09:21.760
Eventually, when the chip industry enters another&nbsp;
boom cycle, Samsung should naturally recover. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.760 --> 00:09:26.920
here’s an interesting point to consider: not all&nbsp;
of the chip industry is getting destroyed. In&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.920 --> 00:09:31.840
fact, certain sectors within the chip industry&nbsp;
are doing better than ever specifically AI. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.840 --> 00:09:36.040
don’t even need to explain this, I mean,&nbsp;
just take a look at Nvidia’s stock graph,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.040 --> 00:09:41.560
it’s vertical. Granted the stock market is very&nbsp;
much being irrational with Nvidia but it’s not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.560 --> 00:09:46.760
like Nvidia’s doing bad fundamentally either. In&nbsp;
fact, they just posted their largest quarterly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.760 --> 00:09:52.640
profit of all time coming in at $6 billion.&nbsp;
Of course, Nvidia is an exception case who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.640 --> 00:09:57.840
has largely benefited from the AI boom. Everyone&nbsp;
focused on traditional chip making like Samsung,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.840 --> 00:10:05.560
TSMC, AMD, and Intel have seen their margins get&nbsp;
obliterated, but this brings up a larger question:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.560 --> 00:10:11.680
where exactly does Samsung fit within the era of&nbsp;
peak smartphone. While it’s true that Samsung has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.680 --> 00:10:16.160
made massive advancements with mobile chips and&nbsp;
memory over the past 15 years, a lot of their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.160 --> 00:10:21.480
success is actually just thanks to the success&nbsp;
of the smartphone industry at large. This was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.480 --> 00:10:27.520
great in the early to mid 2010s when smartphones&nbsp;
sales were exploding, but over the past 5 years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.520 --> 00:10:32.760
smartphone sales have started to fall quite&nbsp;
noticeably. And the more time that passes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.760 --> 00:10:38.760
the more likely it feels that smartphones are&nbsp;
destined for the same fate as PCs. As PC sales&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.760 --> 00:10:43.960
started to fall, manufacturers naturally started&nbsp;
competing on price. Of course, there were some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.960 --> 00:10:49.560
exceptions like Apple and some higher end product&nbsp;
lines from Dell and Asus. But, for the most part,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.560 --> 00:10:55.320
the PC industry became a race to the bottom and&nbsp;
profit magins edged closer and closer to 0. Many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.320 --> 00:11:00.680
reputable manufacturers like Sony and Toshiba&nbsp;
actually just exited the business altogether.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.680 --> 00:11:05.400
And many of the survivors like Dell and HP started&nbsp;
focusing on other parts of their business like the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.400 --> 00:11:11.080
cloud and servers. They simply kept around their&nbsp;
laptops and PCs as a way to maintain their brand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.080 --> 00:11:15.880
presence as opposed to drive massive profits.&nbsp;
This same thing is starting to happen with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.880 --> 00:11:21.280
smartphone brands as well. In early 2021, for&nbsp;
example, LG left the smartphone business after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:21.280 --> 00:11:26.520
losing money for 23 consecutive quarters.&nbsp;
The same thing happened to HTC, Microsoft,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.520 --> 00:11:32.160
and Nokia as well. Heck even Apple isn’t immune.&nbsp;
In fact, that’s why they stopped reporting iPhone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.160 --> 00:11:37.440
sales 5 years ago. And with how disappointing&nbsp;
the iPhone 15 is, who knows how bad sales will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.440 --> 00:11:42.560
be. But, with that being said, Apple will do&nbsp;
just fine because they’ve shifted their focus to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.560 --> 00:11:48.760
monetizing the use of the iPhone as opposed to the&nbsp;
purchase of the iPhone. The same, however, cannot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:48.760 --> 00:11:54.560
be said about Samsung. Samsung phones run Android&nbsp;
meaning that it’s mainly Google that profits from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:54.560 --> 00:12:00.800
the use of Samsung’s 2 billion phones. Samsung is&nbsp;
still very much dependent on the sales of their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:00.800 --> 00:12:06.080
own smartphones and other smartphones for which&nbsp;
they make internal components. And as smartphones&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:06.080 --> 00:12:12.040
become more and more commoditized, this business&nbsp;
will become less and less viable. This isn’t to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:12.040 --> 00:12:17.000
say that Samsung is on the brink of collapse or&nbsp;
something but the point is that this isn’t just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:17.000 --> 00:12:23.120
a downturn for Samsung. Rather it’s very much a&nbsp;
test regarding whether Samsung can grow and evolve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:23.120 --> 00:12:28.920
past smartphones. Can they capitalize on the AI&nbsp;
trend? Can they shift to monetizing the use of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:28.920 --> 00:12:33.760
Samsung phones? Can they reduce their dependency&nbsp;
on smartphone components? These are the concerns&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:33.760 --> 00:12:38.480
that Samsung will have to address in the coming&nbsp;
years if they want to grow past the era of peak&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:38.480 --> 00:12:44.920
smartphone. Will Samsung be able to make this jump&nbsp;
or will they end up like the next IBM? Only time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:44.920 --> 00:12:49.760
will tell. If you’d like to take advantage of&nbsp;
high interest rates and have the government and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:49.760 --> 00:12:56.080
top companies pay you 4-7%, check out our upcoming&nbsp;
bond investing app Silo in the description below.

